    "md": "**Phaedo tells the story.** The *Phaedo* is narrated: Phaedo, who was there, tells Echecrates (a Pythagorean in Phlius) about Socrates' last day. So there are two layers: Phaedo's narrative (ἡμεῖς 'we', ὦ Ἐχέκρατες) and the words spoken in the prison, marked only by **ἔφη** 'he said' and **ἦ δ’ ὅς** 'said he', tucked into the first words of the speech:\n\n| Greek | who speaks |\n|---|---|\n| εἰκότως γε, **ἔφη**, ὦ Κρίτων … | Socrates |\n| οὐδὲν ἄλλο, **ἔφη**, ἢ πιόντα περιιέναι | the man with the poison |\n| τοσοῦτον, **ἔφη**, ὦ Σώκρατες, τρίβομεν | the man again |\n| μανθάνω, **ἦ δ’ ὅς** | Socrates |\n\nThere are no quotation marks in Greek texts: the vocative (ὦ Κρίτων, ὦ βέλτιστε, ὦ Σώκρατες) and the change of person tell you who is talking.\n\n**καὶ ὅς** 'and he': the relative pronoun used as a demonstrative at the start of a clause, as in ἦ δ’ ὅς. So καὶ ὃς λαβών … 'and he took it …'.\n\n**Moods at the end of a life.** A wish in the optative: γένοιτο ταύτῃ 'may it be so'. Commands in the imperative: ἴθι, πείθου, μὴ ἄλλως ποίει; ἡσυχίαν ἄγετε καὶ καρτερεῖτε. Purpose in secondary sequence: ἀπέπεμψα ἵνα μὴ … πλημμελοῖεν (Unit 9). Result: ὥστε ἐγκαλυψάμενος ἀπέκλαον ἐμαυτόν (Unit 10).\n\n**Two perfects of drinking.** πίνοντά τε καὶ **πεπωκότα** 'drinking and having drunk': the perfect participle marks the finished act that cannot be undone.",
    "paradigms": [
      "eimi-go",
      "luo"
    ],
    "diagram": "diagram-moods"
  },
