  "culture": {
    "title": "The death of Socrates",
    "md": "Socrates was executed in the spring of **399 BC**, about a month after his trial (the sacred ship's voyage to Delos delayed it, as you read in 12.3). The method was **κώνειον**, poison hemlock, ground and mixed with water: an Athenian way of execution for citizens that the condemned drank himself. Plato's account of numbness rising from the feet matches, in the main, what is known of the plant's effects, though doctors still debate how gentle such a death can be.\n\nThe *Phaedo* is the dialogue of that last day. Most of it is argument: Socrates tries to prove to his Pythagorean friends Simmias and Cebes that the soul (ψυχή) is immortal, and ends with a myth of the world of the dead. Then he bathes, sends away his wife Xanthippe, his three sons and the women of the family, and drinks. **Phaedo** of Elis, a young man who had been a prisoner of war and a slave before Socrates' friends bought his freedom, tells the story to **Echecrates** at Phlius in the Peloponnese. Plato names himself only to say that he was ill and absent.\n\nThe public prison of Athens has been sought in the south-west corner of the Agora, where excavators found a building with cells and, in a cistern, thirteen small clay medicine bottles. Whether it is Socrates' prison is uncertain; the Agora Museum in the Stoa of Attalos displays the finds.",
    "image": "phil-culture-6-hemlock"
  },
